From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: pin pm6125 L24 (UFS VCC) at 2.96 V

Same story as L11 (UFS VCCQ2), the other UFS rail. The &rpm_requests block
came from sm6375-sony-xperia-murray-pdx225.dts, so L24 carries Sony's wide
range, 2704 mV to 3544 mV. Mainline's RPM regulator driver never reads the
hardware, and the only consumer of this rail is ufshcd, which calls
regulator_enable() and regulator_set_load() over vcc but never
regulator_set_voltage(). So the framework settles on the range's floor. With
a min of 2704000 against the pm660_pldo660 range (base 1504000, 8 mV steps)
the selector lands on 2704000 uV exactly.

Measured on the device before this build:

	l24    enabled   2704000 uV

L24 is UFS VCC, the main memory rail:

	ufshc  vcc-supply = <&pm6125_l24>

2.704 V is below the 2.96 V the storage wants. The part is SK Hynix
H9HQ15AECMBDAR; VCC below its operating point degrades program/erase margins
in the NAND -- incomplete writes/erases, higher BER and worse retention -- so
it is a data-integrity risk on the memory itself, not just the link. Less
acute than VCCQ2 under its floor (fixed in the previous patch) but real.

The vendor pins it with qcom,init-voltage = <2960000> (L24A) in
holi-regulators-pm6125.dtsi; mainline has no such property, so the constraint
itself has to say it. 2960000 is on the 8 mV grid (selector 182), so no
rounding surprise. Narrowing to min == max is what makes the framework vote
2.96 V, the same fix applied to L9 (v125) and L11 (v127).

Not yet confirmed by measurement of the physical rail; a fix by inspection
against the vendor tree. UFS enumerated clean and a 64 MiB write/read past
dropped caches matched after the L11 fix, which is the regression to repeat
here.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -366,9 +366,18 @@
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <3312000>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * 2.96 V, not the 2704 mV the bottom of the PMIC's range gives.
+		 *
+		 * L24 is UFS VCC, the NAND's main rail. ufshcd only enables it
+		 * (it never votes a voltage), so the framework picks the range
+		 * minimum, 2704 mV -- below the 2.96 V the storage operates at.
+		 * The vendor pins it with qcom,init-voltage = <2960000>, which
+		 * mainline has no equivalent for.
+		 */
 		pm6125_l24: l24 {
-			regulator-min-microvolt = <2704000>;
-			regulator-max-microvolt = <3544000>;
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <2960000>;
+			regulator-max-microvolt = <2960000>;
 		};
 	};
 
